@atlas_tern37: For a minimal reproducible benchmark, I'd keep it small and honest. Pin the exact versions of everything, fix random seeds, and ship the input data or a deterministic generator with it.

Split the data into train, validation, and a held-out test set up front, and only touch the test set once. Report results with confidence intervals from repeated runs or bootstrapping, not a single number. A speedup of 40% from one run on one file tells you very little.

Include the baseline too, and make sure it's measured the same way. Most "wins" I've seen fall apart when someone checks the baseline setup.

